ive diagnosis and treatment.\n\n## Cowden syndrome's genetic basis\n\nCowden syndrome is primarily caused by mutations in the PTEN gene, which plays a crucial role in regulating cell growth and apoptosis. This genetic condition is associated with an increased risk of various cancers, including breast, thyroid, and endometrial cancers. Understanding the genetic basis of Cowden syndrome is essential for diagnosis and management, as approximately 80% of cases involve germline mutations in the PTEN gene. This highlights the critical role of PTEN in both the pathology of the syndrome and the identification of at-risk individuals. [Data: Entities (4708, 4710); Relationships (13956, 13968)]\n\n## Significance of PTEN in Cowden syndrome\n\nThe PTEN gene is integral to the development of Cowden syndrome, as mutations in this gene are fundamental for the syndrome's diagnosis. PTEN functions as a tumor suppressor, and its mutations lead to uncontrolled cell growth, resulting in the formation of hamartomas and an increased cancer risk. The relationship between Cowden syndrome and PTEN underscores the importance of genetic testing for individuals with a family history of the syndrome, as early detection can significantly impact management strategies. [Data: Entities (4710, 4708); Relationships (13968, 13956)]\n\n## Associated health risks of Cowden syndrome\n\nIndividuals with Cowden syndrome face a heightened risk of developing several types of cancer, particularly breast, thyroid, and endometrial cancers. The syndrome is characterized by the presence of hamartomas, which are benign tumors that can occur in various tissues. The management of patients with Cowden syndrome often includes regular screenings and preventive measures to mitigate cancer risks, emphasizing the need for awareness and education regarding the syndrome's implications. [Data: Entities (4708, 4714); Relationships (13985)]\n\n## Interrelation with other genetic syndromes\n\nCowden syndrome shares similarities